types.description:
RpcAnnotation declares an RPC's call surface on two independent axes plus a
 set of non-exclusive protocol flags. It is per-RPC: the axes are properties
 of the call, not of the service that happens to host it.

 Example: option (spec.rpc) = {principal: RPC_PRINCIPAL_SERVICE};
 Example: option (spec.rpc) = {
            principal: RPC_PRINCIPAL_USER,
            exposure: RPC_EXPOSURE_EXTERNAL,
            flags: [RPC_FLAG_AUTH_ENTITLED_USERS]
          };

types.schema_json:
{
  "name": "RpcAnnotation",
  "package": "spec",
  "description": "RpcAnnotation declares an RPC's call surface on two independent axes plus a\n set of non-exclusive protocol flags. It is per-RPC: the axes are properties\n of the call, not of the service that happens to host it.\n\n Example: option (spec.rpc) = {principal: RPC_PRINCIPAL_SERVICE};\n Example: option (spec.rpc) = {\n            principal: RPC_PRINCIPAL_USER,\n            exposure: RPC_EXPOSURE_EXTERNAL,\n            flags: [RPC_FLAG_AUTH_ENTITLED_USERS]\n          };",
  "fields": [
    {
      "name": "principal",
      "type": "enum",
      "description": "principal is required; RPC_PRINCIPAL_UNSPECIFIED is not a valid declaration.",
      "enum_values": [
        {
          "name": "RPC_PRINCIPAL_USER",
          "number": 1,
          "description": "RPC_PRINCIPAL_USER: the caller acts as a user identity. Internally that is\n a client calling as a given user; externally it is the actual user."
        },
        {
          "name": "RPC_PRINCIPAL_SERVICE",
          "number": 2,
          "description": "RPC_PRINCIPAL_SERVICE: the caller acts as a service identity."
        },
        {
          "name": "RPC_PRINCIPAL_MCP",
          "number": 3,
          "description": "RPC_PRINCIPAL_MCP: this RPC is declared as an MCP tool rather than a gRPC\n call. tacplus generates the tool schema and a handler interface method the\n service implements by hand; it maps the tool to no gRPC RPC of its own.\n Being a value of this enum rather than a flag is what keeps a DECLARED tool\n from also being a gRPC call: an rpc block holds one principal.\n\n It is NOT how most tools come to exist. Every user RPC of a service_c2s\n service — not RPC_PRINCIPAL_SERVICE, minus RPC_FLAG_PUBLIC_NO_AUTH and\n RPC_FLAG_SELF_AUTHENTICATES — is published as a tool automatically, with no\n annotation asking for one and with its gRPC call, REST route and\n entitlement grant intact. Declare this principal only for a tool that has\n no gRPC rpc of its own. Authorization is unchanged either way — flags /\n auth_services / target_namespace_field mean the same thing against whatever\n Caller the MCP session captured at handshake as they do against a gRPC\n caller.\n\n Streaming is a hard generation failure on this principal: the tool-call wire\n format has no representation for a stream. A handler is free to drive a\n Watch internally and fold it into one response."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "name": "exposure",
      "type": "enum",
      "description": "exposure defaults to RPC_EXPOSURE_INTERNAL.",
      "enum_values": [
        {
          "name": "RPC_EXPOSURE_INTERNAL",
          "number": 0,
          "description": "RPC_EXPOSURE_INTERNAL: reachable only from inside the cluster boundary."
        },
        {
          "name": "RPC_EXPOSURE_EXTERNAL",
          "number": 1,
          "description": "RPC_EXPOSURE_EXTERNAL: reachable from outside the cluster boundary."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "name": "flags",
      "type": "enum",
      "description": "flags is an unordered set; RPC_FLAG_UNSPECIFIED is not a valid entry.",
      "repeated": true,
      "constraints": [
        "maximum size: 1024 elements (default)"
      ],
      "constraint_infos": [
        {
          "rule": "default_max_size",
          "target": "field",
          "value_kind": "int",
          "int_value": 1024,
          "defaulted": true,
          "unit": "elements",
          "source": "default_bound"
        }
      ],
      "enum_values": [
        {
          "name": "RPC_FLAG_PUBLIC_NO_AUTH",
          "number": 1,
          "description": "RPC_FLAG_PUBLIC_NO_AUTH: this RPC is a public endpoint that requires no\n caller token at all (OIDC discovery, JWKS). Nothing authenticates the\n caller, because there is nothing to authenticate."
        },
        {
          "name": "RPC_FLAG_AUTH_ANY_USER",
          "number": 2,
          "description": "RPC_FLAG_AUTH_ANY_USER: any authenticated user may call, with no\n entitlement check (e.g. token exchange, event streams)."
        },
        {
          "name": "RPC_FLAG_AUTH_ENTITLED_USERS",
          "number": 3,
          "description": "RPC_FLAG_AUTH_ENTITLED_USERS: only users holding a matching entitlement\n may call. This is today's default policy for user-facing RPCs."
        },
        {
          "name": "RPC_FLAG_SELF_AUTHENTICATES",
          "number": 4,
          "description": "RPC_FLAG_SELF_AUTHENTICATES: this RPC carries no bearer token because it\n authenticates the caller from the request body itself (bootstrap token\n minting, cred-pair JWT). The caller interceptor skips it; the handler does\n the authentication. Distinct from RPC_FLAG_PUBLIC_NO_AUTH: the caller is\n still authenticated, just not by the token path."
        },
        {
          "name": "RPC_FLAG_MINTS_CREDENTIAL",
          "number": 5,
          "description": "RPC_FLAG_MINTS_CREDENTIAL: this RPC's response mints or returns a live\n credential — a token, a certificate, a delegate grant — so it is never\n auto-exposed as an MCP tool, whatever its principal. It carries no\n auth/authorizer semantics of its own: a mint RPC's authorization is\n already fully expressed by its principal, its other flags and its\n auth_services. This flag only affects MCP auto-exposure."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "name": "auth_services",
      "type": "string",
      "description": "auth_services lists the service identities allowed to call this RPC.\n [\"*\"] = any authenticated service. Per-RPC: two RPCs of one service may\n legitimately admit different callers.\n\n On an RPC_PRINCIPAL_USER rpc of a service_c2s service it ADDS the listed\n service identities to the users the rpc already admits: a user token is\n still authorized by its entitlements, a service token by these patterns,\n and a service not listed is refused. This is how one rpc is \"c2s for\n users + s2s for these services\"; the handler's Caller is whichever\n identity the verified token names.",
      "repeated": true,
      "constraints": [
        "maximum size: 1024 elements (default)",
        "maximum element size: 32768 bytes (default)"
      ],
      "constraint_infos": [
        {
          "rule": "default_max_size",
          "target": "field",
          "value_kind": "int",
          "int_value": 1024,
          "defaulted": true,
          "unit": "elements",
          "source": "default_bound"
        },
        {
          "rule": "default_max_element_size",
          "target": "element",
          "value_kind": "int",
          "int_value": 32768,
          "defaulted": true,
          "unit": "bytes",
          "source": "default_bound"
        }
